# Python  
__pycache__/  
*.py[cod]  
*$py.class  
  
# Jupyter Notebook  
.ipynb_checkpoints  
  
# Virtual Environment  
env/  
venv/  
.venv/  
.env  
pip-log.txt  
pip-delete-this-directory.txt  
  
# IDE  
.idea/  
.vscode/  
*.swp  
*.swo  
  
# Logs and databases  
logs/  
database.log  
*.log  
  
# OS generated files  
.DS_Store  
Thumbs.db  
  
# Build files  
build/  
dist/  
*.egg-info/  
__pycache__/  
.coverage  
.coverage.*  
htmlcov/  
  
# Environment variables file  
.env 
# User-supplied model weights and whisper.cpp binaries.
# Ignored wholesale rather than file-by-file: a single ggml model is ~250 MB,
# well past GitHub's 100 MB per-file push limit, and listing binaries
# individually meant any newly added .dll slipped through. Both folders are
# documented in README.md / SETUP_AMD.md as things you download yourself.
# _models/* rather than _models/ : git does not descend into an ignored
# DIRECTORY, so a negation for anything inside one can never match. Ignoring
# the contents instead is what lets the small Silero export below be kept.
_models/*
!_models/silero_vad*.onnx
_whisper.cpp/
_whisper.cpp.bck/

# Saved transcripts (--save; auto-named transcript_YYYYmmdd_HHMMSS.txt)
transcript_*.txt
my_transcript.txt

# Saved presets are personal setups; the folder is kept, its contents are not.
# presets/builtin/ IS tracked, deliberately: it holds the shipped VAD profiles
# that give the panels the same Speech / Music / Off choice run_pipeline.cmd
# offers. No negation rule is needed for them, because a gitignore * does not
# cross a / - the pattern below only ever matched direct children. Saving a
# preset under a built-in's name writes an untracked file here that shadows
# it, so your own setups can never dirty the checkout.
presets/*.json

# The panel's one remembered value (settings.REMEMBERED - currently just the
# GGML model the engine last started on). Untracked because it is a note about
# THIS machine's _models folder, not a setting the project ships: a name that
# is meaningful here is a file nobody else has. Deleting it restores the
# defaults, and its absence is the ordinary first-run case.
_state.json

# ============================================================
#  Desktop panel (ui/) - .NET
#
#  ui/runtime IS committed, deliberately. It is 6.4 MB of publish output and
#  it is what lets a fresh clone run the panel with only the .NET Desktop
#  runtime - no SDK, no NuGet restore, no network. The alternative is a fetch
#  script that costs every cloner the SDK. Re-run build_ui.cmd and commit the
#  result after changing anything under ui/.
#
#  Everything else .NET produces is per-machine and must stay out.
# ============================================================
ui/bin/
ui/obj/
ui/**/bin/
ui/**/obj/

# Debug symbols: per-build, and of no use to someone running the panel.
ui/runtime/*.pdb

# Visual Studio / Rider per-user state.
.vs/
*.user
*.suo
*.userprefs

# Default output of ui/tools/shot.py when no path is given: one PNG per tab
# (panel-transcript.png ... panel-log.png, panel-light-*.png), or panel.png
# with --tab.
/panel*.png

# Generated by tests/make_speech_sample.py (Windows SAPI); the real-model
# tests skip themselves without it. Not committed: 800 KB of WAV, and it is
# reproducible.
tests/fixtures/

# Claude Code's per-checkout state. settings.local.json is machine-local
# permissions, and worktrees/ is where agent worktrees are created - both are
# this checkout's business and neither belongs to a cloner. Covered here
# rather than only in .git/info/exclude, which is local and is NOT shared, so
# without this rule every clone rediscovers the same untracked noise.
.claude/
